      <h2 id="vessels">Cups, bottles, and temperature</h2>
      <p>The standard serving vessel is a small ceramic or glass cup holding 15–25 ml — roughly a third of a shot. Restaurants use them because a large pour of 52% spirit goes warm and flat before you finish it, and because the small format is designed for many refills across a long meal rather than one big drink.</p>
      <p>Two upgrades are worth making at home. First, a <strong>tulip-shaped glass</strong> — the same shape used for whisky — concentrates aroma far better than an open cup and makes the difference between two bottles much easier to read. Second, a <strong>small decanter or pitcher</strong> (分酒器), which is how Chinese tables actually serve: the bottle stays sealed and the pitcher gets passed around.</p>
      <p><strong>Temperature: room, always.</strong> Chilling baijiu suppresses exactly the aromatic compounds you paid for. Ice dilutes and numbs at the same time. If a 52% bottle feels too hot, the answer is a smaller sip or a lower-ABV expression — Yanghe's 40.8% or Luzhou Laojiao's 38% — not refrigeration.</p>
      <p>One nuance: in winter, some drinkers warm the cup gently in hot water. This is traditional for certain styles and does soften the alcohol, but it also volatilises the aromatics quickly. Try it once with a cheap bottle to see the effect; do not do it with an expensive one.</p>

