      <h2 id="taste">How to actually taste it</h2>
      <p>Baijiu tasting follows the same four steps as any serious spirit, but the emphasis is different: aroma carries more of the information than the palate does.</p>

      <h3>1. Look — briefly</h3>
      <p>Baijiu is clear to faintly straw. Colour tells you almost nothing: unlike whisky, dark colour is not a sign of age, and any tint is usually from the jar or a deliberate addition. A slight oiliness on the glass wall is normal and indicates body. Do not spend long here.</p>

      <h3>2. Nose — slowly, at a distance</h3>
      <p>Hold the cup at chin height and breathe normally. Do not put your nose in it and inhale hard; at 52% you will get alcohol burn and lose the aromatics behind it. Then move closer and take two or three short sniffs. Wait ten seconds. The second pass is usually where the real character appears — the fruit in a strong aroma, the soy and mushroom in a sauce aroma, the pear in a light aroma.</p>
      <p>Do not swirl. Swirling is a whisky habit that works because whisky is 40% and lower; agitating a 52% spirit releases ethanol faster than aroma and flattens the experience.</p>

      <h3>3. Palate — small, and hold</h3>
      <p>Take 5 ml or so and let it sit on the front of your tongue for two seconds before swallowing. The first impression is often heat; the flavour arrives behind it. Note three things: how sweet it reads, how viscous it feels, and where the heat lands. A well-made baijiu puts the heat at the back of the throat and keeps the front of the palate sweet. A rough one burns across the whole mouth immediately.</p>

      <h3>4. Finish — and the second sip</h3>
      <p>Count how long the flavour lasts after you swallow. Entry-level bottles fade in ten seconds. A good strong aroma holds thirty. A serious sauce aroma can run past a minute and change character on the way — savoury first, then a returning sweetness. The second sip is always more informative than the first, because the palate has adjusted.</p>
      <blockquote><p>If a baijiu tastes harsh, try it again after a bite of food. In our experience most "harsh" bottles are simply being drunk in the wrong context.</p></blockquote>

