      <h3>Pairing rules that generalise</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Fat is your friend.</strong> Pork belly, lamb, duck skin — fat coats the palate and makes high-ABV spirit read as smooth rather than sharp.</li>
        <li><strong>Acid resets.</strong> Vinegar dishes, pickles, and cold cucumber revive the palate between sips and let you taste the next glass properly.</li>
        <li><strong>Chilli needs sugar.</strong> Strong aroma's fruit-forward sweetness is the reason it survives Sichuan food. Sauce aroma with extreme chilli can turn metallic.</li>
        <li><strong>Do not pair with delicate fish</strong> unless the baijiu is light or rice aroma. A 52% sauce aroma will erase a steamed bass entirely.</li>
        <li><strong>Western food works too.</strong> Roast lamb, hard cheese, charcuterie, and smoked brisket all pair well with strong and sauce aroma. Avoid cream sauces and anything heavily sweetened.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="banquet">Banquet etiquette without the damage</h2>
      <p>Chinese banquet drinking is a social system, not a drinking competition. Understanding the structure removes most of the anxiety — and most of the pressure.</p>

      <h3>The seating logic</h3>
      <p>At a formal round table, the host (主陪) sits facing the door. The guest of honour (主宾) sits to the host's right. The co-host (副陪) sits directly opposite the host, and is usually the one who drinks most and keeps the table moving. If you are a guest, you will be placed; do not rearrange.</p>

      <h3>The toast sequence</h3>
      <p>A formal meal opens with a host's welcome — often three glasses together, or three sips from one. After that, toasts proceed by seniority: the host to the guest of honour, then the guest of honour returns it, then others join in order. When you clink, <strong>your rim should sit slightly lower than the other person's</strong>, particularly if they are older or more senior. It is a small gesture that is noticed.</p>
      <p><em>Ganbei</em> (干杯) means empty the glass. <em>Suíyì</em> (随意) means drink as you please. If a host says 随意, take it literally — that is the polite escape hatch, and using it is not rude. If someone says 干杯, you are not obligated to match the volume; matching the gesture is what counts.</p>

      <h3>What is actually expected of you</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Participate in the first round.</strong> Skipping the opening toast entirely reads as coldness.</li>
        <li><strong>Return a toast.</strong> If someone drinks to you individually, drink back at some point. It does not need to be the same glass.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep your glass no fuller than half.</strong> A full glass makes every subsequent 干杯 harder.</li>
        <li><strong>Eat between toasts.</strong> This is not optional for pacing — it is the only thing that makes a two-hour banquet survivable.</li>
        <li><strong>You do not have to finish every glass.</strong> Leaving a little is normal at a long table; the ritual is the raising, not the volume.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="callout"><strong>Business dinners:</strong> the pressure is highest here, and the expectation is usually participation rather than volume. A guest who drinks moderately, toasts everyone once, and stays coherent is remembered better than one who tries to keep pace with the co-host.</div>

      <h2 id="decline">How to decline a drink politely</h2>
      <p>Refusing alcohol in a Chinese banquet context needs a reason, not a preference. "I don't want to" is socially expensive. The standard moves:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Health, stated plainly.</strong> 我最近在吃药，不能喝酒 ("I'm on medication, I can't drink") is accepted without follow-up. So is 医生不让 ("doctor's orders").</li>
        <li><strong>Driving.</strong> 我开车来的 ("I'm driving") is now widely respected, and enforcement in China is strict enough that no one argues.</li>
        <li><strong>Tea substitution.</strong> 以茶代酒 — offer to toast with tea instead. Hold the teacup the same way, make the same gesture. This is a recognised, face-saving solution and hosts accept it routinely.</li>
        <li><strong>Pregnancy, religion, allergy.</strong> All accepted immediately; no explanation needed.</li>
        <li><strong>Nurse the glass.</strong> If you want to avoid the conversation entirely, keep a partially full glass and take small sips. Nobody refills a glass that is not empty, and nobody toasts someone whose glass is already raised.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>What not to do: pretend to drink and pour it away visibly, or agree to a 干杯 and then not drink. Both are read as disrespect rather than moderation. Decline clearly at the start — it is far easier than declining repeatedly through the evening.</p>

      <h2 id="cocktails">Baijiu in cocktails</h2>
      <p>Baijiu works in mixed drinks, but only if you treat it as a flavoured spirit rather than a neutral base. The substitution rule: use light or rice aroma where a recipe calls for vodka or gin, and use strong or sauce aroma where a recipe calls for an aged or smoky spirit.</p>

      <h3>Three ratios that work</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Baijiu sour.</strong> 45 ml light aroma (Fenjiu) + 20 ml lemon juice + 15 ml syrup + 1 egg white. Shake hard. The clean grain behaves like a high-proof gin and the acid carries it.</li>
        <li><strong>Baijiu highball.</strong> 45 ml rice aroma + 90 ml soda + a wide lemon peel, over one large cube. The softness of rice aroma makes this genuinely refreshing — the best gateway drink for sceptics.</li>
        <li><strong>Sauce-aroma old fashioned.</strong> 45 ml sauce aroma + 7 ml syrup + 2 dashes aromatic bitters, stirred, orange peel. Use a $30 bottle, not Moutai. The savoury notes read like a peated whisky and the drink is excellent.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>What does not work: sauce aroma in anything with citrus-forward or creamy ingredients. The fermented notes turn sharp and the drink collapses. Also avoid using baijiu as a float or a rinse — its intensity is too high for that role.</p>

      <h2 id="storage">Storing an open bottle</h2>
      <p>Baijiu does not spoil, but it does fade. Once air is in the bottle, esters slowly oxidise and the aroma flattens — usually noticeable after six to twelve months in a half-empty bottle.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Keep it upright.</strong> High-ABV spirit degrades cork and cap liners if it sits against them.</li>
        <li><strong>Reduce headspace.</strong> Decant the remainder into a smaller bottle. This is the single most effective step.</li>
        <li><strong>Dark, stable, cool.</strong> Not the fridge — temperature swings are worse than a steady room. A cupboard away from the stove is fine.</li>
        <li><strong>Seal the cap with film</strong> if you plan to keep it more than a year.</li>
        <li><strong>Do not store in the gift box.</strong> Silk and foam linings hold smells that the spirit will pick up through the closure.</li>
      </ul>

      <section class="faq" id="faq">
        <h2>Frequently asked</h2>
        <h3>Should baijiu be chilled or served over ice?</h3>
        <p>No. Chilling suppresses the aromatic compounds that define the spirit, and ice dilutes it while numbing the palate. Room temperature in a small cup is the correct serve. If the alcohol heat is too much, choose a lower-ABV expression instead.</p>
        <h3>Do I have to finish every toast?</h3>
        <p>No. Chinese toasting has real conventions, but they are about participation and gesture rather than volume. Keep your glass half full, eat between rounds, and use 以茶代酒 (toast with tea) if you need to stop. Clear early refusal is respected; repeated half-compliance is not.</p>
        <h3>Why do Chinese cups look so small?</h3>
        <p>Because the format is designed for a long meal with many refills, not a single large drink. A 20 ml pour of 52% spirit delivers more aromatic impact than a 50 ml pour of 40% vodka, and it stays at the right temperature while you eat.</p>
        <h3>Can I drink baijiu with Western food?</h3>
        <p>Yes. Strong and sauce aroma work well with roast lamb, hard cheese, charcuterie, and smoked meat. Avoid cream sauces, sweet desserts, and anything delicate — a 52% sauce aroma will erase a light fish dish.</p>
        <h3>Does baijiu give a worse hangover than other spirits?</h3>
        <p>There is no strong evidence that it does, but several factors make it feel that way: the ABV is often higher than Western spirits, the pours are frequent, and it is usually drunk over a long meal with rich food. Congeners — the compounds behind the aroma — are present at high levels, and congeners are associated with worse hangovers. The practical answer is the same as for any spirit: smaller pours, more food, more water.</p>
        <h3>Is it rude to add water?</h3>
        <p>Not rude, but unusual. A few drops of room-temperature water will open up a high-ABV sauce aroma the way it does with cask-strength whisky, and no one will object if you explain what you are doing. Ice is a different matter and will draw comments.</p>
